@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Bridging a Callback API") {
        Legacy APIs such as `CLLocationManager`, `AVAudioSession`, and `URLSessionDownloadTask` deliver
        values through delegate methods or completion handlers rather than `async/await`. In this
        tutorial you will wrap the delegate callbacks of `CLLocationManager` in a `Flow<CLLocation>`
        with `Flow.callbackFlow`, so the rest of your app can work with a clean, cancellable stream.

        By the end of the tutorial you will have a `LocationTracker` type that starts and stops the
        hardware on demand and tears it down with `awaitClose`. It chooses what happens when a
        collector is slow, ignores values until the user grants permission, and reports failures with
        `ThrowingFlow.callbackFlow`. It also shares one `CLLocationManager` between many collectors.
    }

    @Section(title: "Open a producer scope") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `Flow.callbackFlow` takes a block. FlowKit gives the block a `ProducerScope`, and the block
            runs once for each collector. Inside the block you do three things. You register with the
            callback API. From each callback you call `scope.trySend(_:)`, which never suspends, so it is
            safe in a synchronous delegate method on any thread. Then you park the block on
            `scope.awaitClose(_:)` until the flow ends.

            A small *delegate class* keeps the `CLLocationManager` code apart from `Flow`. The class
            forwards each callback to a closure, and the flow body decides what the closure does.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create `LocationManaging.swift`. The protocol lists the part of `CLLocationManager`
                that the tracker uses. The real manager conforms to it, and the test double in the
                last section conforms to it as well.

                @Code(name: "LocationManaging.swift", file: 03-01-protocol.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Create `LocationTracker.swift`. The factory makes the manager on the main actor,
                because `CLLocationManager` delivers delegate callbacks on the run loop of the thread
                that made it. `locationUpdates()` returns a cold `Flow<CLLocation>` that you build
                with `Flow.callbackFlow`. For now the block only closes the scope.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-02-stub.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Create `LocationDelegate.swift`. It conforms to `CLLocationManagerDelegate`. Each
                `locationManager(_:didUpdateLocations:)` call passes the last location to a closure.
                The class is `Sendable`, because the closure it holds is `@Sendable`.

                @Code(name: "LocationDelegate.swift", file: 03-03-delegate.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Fill in the block. Make the manager, create the delegate with a closure that calls
                `scope.trySend(_:)`, attach the delegate, and start the updates. The block is
                still wrong. It returns at the end, and you will see why in the next section.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-04-register.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Tear down with awaitClose") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A `callbackFlow` block that returns completes the flow. The listing in the last step
            returns right after `startUpdatingLocation()`. The collector sees the flow end at once.
            The `CLLocationManager` keeps its delegate in a weak reference, so the delegate is
            released and the manager runs with nobody to listen. In debug builds `callbackFlow`
            catches this mistake with an assertion, because the block returned without a call to
            `awaitClose` or `close()`. In release builds you get no warning.

            `awaitClose` fixes it. It suspends the block until the flow closes. The flow closes when
            the collector is cancelled, when the collection ends, or when the block calls `close()`.
            Then `awaitClose` runs your closure exactly one time. That closure is the place to
            unregister the callback.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                End the block with `await scope.awaitClose { ... }`. The closure stops the manager and
                clears the delegate. It also holds on to the delegate, so the delegate stays alive for as
                long as the block is parked. Now cancelling the collector task stops the hardware. No
                `withTaskCancellationHandler` and no hand-written continuation are needed.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-05-await-close.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Choose what a slow collector sees") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Every `callbackFlow` and `channelFlow` has a buffer between the producer and the
            collector. It takes two parameters. `bufferCapacity` is how many unconsumed values the
            buffer holds, and it is `64` by default. A capacity of zero or less means unbounded.
            `onBufferOverflow` is what a full buffer does with a new value.

            - `.dropOldest` is the default. It evicts the oldest value and keeps the newest.
            - `.dropLatest` keeps what is in the buffer and discards the new value.
            - `.suspend` backs up the producer. Only `send(_:)` can wait. `trySend(_:)` cannot wait, so
              under `.suspend` it buffers without a bound.

            `trySend(_:)` returns a `ChannelSendResult`: `.enqueued`, `.dropped` for a full
            `.dropLatest` buffer, or `.closed` after the flow has ended.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                A location is a signal where only the newest value matters. Set `bufferCapacity: 1`
                with `.dropOldest`. A slow collector then always receives the latest fix and never
                reads a backlog of old ones. A delegate callback is synchronous, so it must use
                `trySend(_:)`.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-06-buffer.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                A producer that is `async` can use real backpressure. This replay of a recorded
                track uses `Flow.channelFlow` with `.suspend`. `send(_:)` waits while eight values are
                unconsumed, so a slow collector slows the replay and loses nothing. A `channelFlow`
                block needs no `awaitClose`. It ends when the block returns.

                @Code(name: "TrackReplay.swift", file: 03-07-track-replay.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Handle permission and failure") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            `CLLocationManager` can call `didUpdateLocations` before the user answers the permission
            dialog. It can also fail, or lose permission while the app runs. A `Flow` cannot fail, so
            the first version ends the flow with `close()`. Then `awaitClose` runs the same teardown.

            To tell the collector why the flow ended, use `ThrowingFlow.callbackFlow`. Its block
            receives a `ThrowingProducerScope`, which adds `close(throwing:)`. The collector receives
            every value sent before the call, and then its `collect` throws the error.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Rewrite the delegate. It reads the authorization status and drops updates until
                the user grants permission. `didFailWithError` and `locationManagerDidChangeAuthorization`
                call an `onFailure` closure. The transient `.locationUnknown` error is not a failure,
                because the manager keeps trying.

                @Code(name: "LocationDelegate.swift", file: 03-08-delegate-failure.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Ask for permission before you start updates. Pass the manager to the delegate, and make
                `onFailure` call `scope.close()`. The flow ends, `awaitClose` resumes, and the
                manager stops.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-09-request-permission.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Switch to `ThrowingFlow.callbackFlow` and call `scope.close(throwing:)`. The signature
                of the block is the same. The first close wins, so a later `close()` or error is
                ignored. A cancelled collector is not a failure. Its `collect` returns without an
                error.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-10-throwing.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Share one manager between collectors") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A `callbackFlow` is cold. Each collector runs the block, so two collectors create two
            `CLLocationManager` instances. For hardware you want one. `asSharedFlow` runs the cold flow
            one time and multicasts it. `ThrowingFlow` has no `asSharedFlow`, so `catch` turns it into
            a `Flow` first.

            With `.whileSubscribed(stopTimeout: .zero)` the hardware starts for the first subscriber.
            When the last subscriber leaves, FlowKit cancels the block, and `awaitClose` stops the
            manager.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Expose `locations` as a shared flow with `replay: 1`, so a new subscriber gets the
                most recent location at once. Keep `locationUpdates()` for a caller that wants to see
                the error. The shared flow ends quietly when the cold flow fails.

                @Code(name: "LocationTracker.swift", file: 03-11-share.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Create a `MapViewController` that subscribes to `tracker.locations`. The buffer in
                `callbackFlow` protects the producer. `.keepingLatest()` adds a one-element
                drop-oldest buffer for this collector, so a slow redraw skips old locations.

                @Code(name: "MapViewController.swift", file: 03-12-keepinglatest.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Launch the collection in a `FlowScope` tied to the view controller. When the scope is
                cancelled and this was the last subscriber, the block is cancelled and `awaitClose`
                stops the manager. The view controller needs no teardown code of its own.

                @Code(name: "MapViewController.swift", file: 03-13-scope.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Test the bridge") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Testing real hardware is flaky. The tracker takes a factory, so a test injects a mock and
            drives the delegate by calling it directly. Each test makes its own `LocationTracker`, so
            the one manager of the shared flow is the mock. Each test has a time limit, so a wait
            that never ends fails the test and does not hang it.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write a `MockLocationManager` that stores the delegate. `simulateLocation(_:)` and
                `simulateFailure(_:)` call the delegate methods. `waitUntilStarted()` and
                `waitUntilStopped()` suspend until the tracker starts or stops the manager.

                @Code(name: "MockLocationManager.swift", file: 03-14-mock.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Write the first test. Inject the mock, collect the flow in a `probing` closure, wait
                for the tracker to start the mock, call `simulateLocation`, and assert the emitted
                coordinate.

                @Code(name: "LocationTrackerTests.swift", file: 03-15-test.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Add a cleanup test. When the `probing` closure exits, the collection is cancelled.
                Wait for the mock to stop, which proves that the `awaitClose` closure ran and the
                hardware shut down.

                @Code(name: "LocationTrackerTests.swift", file: 03-16-cleanup-test.swift)
            }

            @Step {
                Add a failure test on `locationUpdates()`. `simulateFailure(_:)` makes the
                collection throw the delegate error, and the manager still stops, because
                `close(throwing:)` also resumes `awaitClose`.

                @Code(name: "LocationTrackerTests.swift", file: 03-17-failure-test.swift)
            }
        }
    }
}
